@page "/markdown/tags"

<PageTitle>Tags in markdown — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="Tags"
           Summary="A subpage of Markdown, about one inline: a `#tag` written in the middle of a sentence. The parser recognises it, the read view draws it as a chip, and the body keeps the literal `#tag` text — a tag is something an author types, not a field they fill in. This page is what makes one, what it renders as, that it does nothing when you press it, and where the tag you can press lives instead. Not to be confused with `roadmap` and `feature-flag`: those are keys inside a `meta` fence, drawn as badges of the feature family, and they belong to the Knowledge base chapter rather than to this one."
           Guidelines="@Rules">

    <Story Name="What makes a tag"
           Description="One alternative of the inline pattern: a `#`, then a letter, then any run of word characters and hyphens. Two things about it do most of the work.

The `#` has to open a word. The pattern is preceded by a negative lookbehind for a non-space character, so a `#` with anything but whitespace in front of it is not a sigil — which is what keeps `issue#42` an issue number and `(#sync)` a bracketed hash. And the character straight after the `#` has to be a letter, which is what keeps `#1` a position and `##heading` two hashes.

Everything else falls out of those two. Case is kept as written, hyphens and underscores continue a tag, and the first character that is neither ends it — so a tag at the end of a sentence leaves its full stop in the prose.

Nothing inside a code span or a fence is inline-parsed at all, so a `#tag` quoted as syntax stays syntax."
           Code="@PatternCode">
        <dl class="sb-grid" data-testid="markdown-tags-rules">
            @foreach (var rule in Rulings)
            {
                <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="@($"markdown-tags-rule-{rule.Slug}")">
                    <dt class="sb-label">@rule.Name — @rule.Verdict</dt>
                    <dd class="sb-stack">
                        <CodeView Source="@rule.Source" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" />
                        <div><MarkdownView Blocks="@MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(rule.Source)" /></div>
                        <p class="sb-state">@rule.Note</p>
                    </dd>
                </div>
            }
        </dl>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="What it renders as, and where it can appear"
           Description="A `span.tag-chip` carrying the literal `#tag` — the hash included, because what the body stores is what the author typed and the chip is only how it is drawn.

It is an inline like any other, so it turns up wherever inlines are parsed — a paragraph, a bullet, a task, a quote, a table cell. It is never a block and never breaks one."
           Code="@RenderCode"
           Source="@PlacesSample">
        <MarkdownStory Source="@PlacesSample" TestId="markdown-tags-places" />
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The chip in a body is not the TagChip component"
           Description="They share the class `tag-chip` and nothing else. The markdown path builds its span by hand in `MarkdownRender` — one `Wrap` call, one element, the text inside it — and never renders `TagChip`. `TagChip` puts its text in an inner `span.tag-chip__label`, turns that label into a `button` when a host wires `OnClick`, and adds an `IconButton.tag-chip__remove` when a host asks for removal. None of those three exists in any body.

So the two have already drifted, and this page is where a reader should find that out rather than working it out from a stylesheet. `.tag-chip` styles the outer box and `.tag-chip__label` styles a child only one of the two draws, which means a rule aimed at the label reaches the component and misses every tag in every body.

It is left that way deliberately. Adopting `TagChip` in `MarkdownRender` would change the markup of every tag in every body this product renders — every entry, every knowledge chapter, every comment — for a component whose two extra features a body has no use for. So this story draws the four live, side by side, and says which is which; the three component forms are documented on Badges, and are here only to be inspected against the inline."
           Code="@DifferenceCode">
        <div class="sb-stack">
            <div class="sb-row" data-testid="markdown-tags-side-by-side">
                <MarkdownView Blocks="@OneTagBlocks" />
                <TagChip Text="#sync" TestId="markdown-tags-component-inline" />
            </div>
            <p class="sb-state">
                The left one came out of the parser. The right one is the component, handed
                the hash by the caller because it does not add one. Same class, same tone,
                different markup underneath — inspect them.
            </p>

            <dl class="sb-grid" data-testid="markdown-tags-difference">
                @foreach (var shape in Shapes)
                {
                    <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="@($"markdown-tags-shape-{shape.Slug}")">
                        <dt class="sb-label">@shape.Name</dt>
                        <dd class="sb-stack">
                            <div class="sb-row">@shape.Draw</div>
                            <p class="sb-state">@shape.Note</p>
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                }
            </dl>
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="markdown-tags-pressed">@PressedSummary</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="A tag in a body does nothing, and where the ones that do live"
           Description="No click, no removal, no tab stop, no `title`. `MarkdownRender` emits the span and stops, and there is nothing to pass it: the inline-target hook the References subpage documents is offered code spans and links only, and a tag is neither. A body is prose that happens to be stored as markdown, and the read view's job is to draw what it says.

Filtering by a tag, adding one and dropping one are all somebody's controls, and they live where controls live. `TagChip` is on Badges — the chip as a component, clickable, removable, disabled. `TagMultiSelect` is on Selects, for choosing several from a set and drawing each chosen one as a `TagChip`. `TaskPanel` is on Task side panel, where a task's tags sit on the surface that owns them and removal writes back.

Linked rather than repeated: each of those pages is the one place its component's behaviour is documented, and a second telling here is a second thing to keep true."
           Code="@InertCode">
        <MarkdownStory Source="@InertSample" TestId="markdown-tags-inert" />
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DesignGuideline[] Rules =
    [
        new("content-editing.md#inline-constructs", "Which inlines the read view recognises, and what a tag is stored as."),
        new("content-editing.md#structured-metadata-sigils", "The `#` sigil, and the four others it is one of."),
        new("color-scheme.md#badge-and-chip-tones", "The tone a chip is allowed to wear.")
    ];

    /// <summary>One line of markdown, what the parser makes of it, and why.</summary>
    private sealed record Ruling(string Name, string Slug, string Source, string Verdict, string Note);

    /// <summary>One tag-shaped thing, drawn live, and what to look for in it.
    /// Live rather than a typed transcript of its markup: nothing keeps a
    /// transcript in step with the component, and the point is to inspect the
    /// real element.</summary>
    private sealed record Shape(string Name, string Slug, RenderFragment Draw, string Note);

    private string? _pressed;

    private string PressedSummary => _pressed is null
        ? "Nothing pressed yet — only the two component forms with a handler can ever land here."
        : $"Last pressed: {_pressed}. What that means is the host's; this page only says which one it was.";

    private void Press(string tag) => _pressed = tag;

    private void ClickTag() => Press("markdown");

    /// <summary>The one tag the comparison row draws, parsed once. A field rather
    /// than a call in the markup, because the markup would need a quoted string
    /// inside a quoted attribute to say it.</summary>
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> OneTagBlocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument("#sync");

    // The four drawings the difference story compares. Templates rather than a
    // switch in the markup: each is one call, and the point of the story is that
    // the four calls are not the same call.
    private RenderFragment DrawInline => @<MarkdownView Blocks="@OneTagBlocks" />;

    private RenderFragment DrawPlain => @<TagChip Text="#sync" />;

    private RenderFragment DrawClickable => @<TagChip Text="#sync" OnClick="@ClickTag" TestId="markdown-tags-clickable" />;

    private RenderFragment DrawRemovable => @<TagChip Text="#sync" Removable="true" OnRemove="@Press" TestId="markdown-tags-removable" />;

    private IReadOnlyList<Shape> Shapes =>
    [
        new("The markdown inline", "markdown", DrawInline,
            "One element, the literal text inside it, hash included. Built by MarkdownRender's MdTag arm — a "
            + "single Wrap call — and not by rendering TagChip. Inspect it: no inner label element."),

        new("TagChip, plain", "component", DrawPlain,
            "An inner label element the markdown path has no equivalent of. The hash is the caller's: TagChip "
            + "prints Text and adds nothing to it."),

        new("TagChip, clickable", "clickable", DrawClickable,
            "The label becomes a button when a host wires OnClick. A tag in a body has no host to wire and no "
            + "parameter to wire it with."),

        new("TagChip, removable", "removable", DrawRemovable,
            "And a second control beside the label. Removing a tag means writing the body back, which is a "
            + "surface's job and not a renderer's.")
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<Ruling> Rulings =
    [
        new("A plain tag", "plain", "Filed under #sync for now.", "a tag",
            "The ordinary case: whitespace in front, a letter straight after the hash."),

        new("Hyphens and underscores", "word-characters", "Both #two-word and #snake_case are one tag each.", "two tags",
            "Word characters and hyphens continue a tag, so neither spelling splits in the middle."),

        new("Case is kept", "case", "The #Sync tag is not the #sync tag as far as this is concerned.", "two tags",
            "Nothing is lower-cased on the way through. What is stored and drawn is what was typed."),

        new("End of a sentence", "punctuation", "That is all for #sync, and for #docs.", "two tags",
            "The first character that is neither a word character nor a hyphen ends the tag, so the comma and "
            + "the full stop stay in the prose where they belong."),

        new("A hash mid-word", "mid-word", "See issue#42 and PR#7 for the rest.", "not a tag",
            "The hash has to open a word. Anything but whitespace in front of it and the pattern does not match "
            + "at all."),

        new("A bracketed hash", "bracketed", "The tag (#sync) is in brackets.", "not a tag",
            "The same rule, and the case most likely to surprise: an opening bracket is not whitespace, so the "
            + "hash after it is prose."),

        new("A number after the hash", "number", "Ranked #1 this week and #2 last week.", "not a tag",
            "The character after the hash has to be a letter. A position is not a tag, which is the whole "
            + "reason for that restriction."),

        new("Two hashes", "double-hash", "##heading without a space is not a heading either.", "not a tag",
            "The second hash is not a letter, and it is not preceded by whitespace. Written with a space after "
            + "the hashes the line would have been a heading block instead of a paragraph."),

        new("A hash that would be a heading", "line-start", "#sync is where this belongs.", "a tag",
            "At the start of a line, and still a tag: a heading needs whitespace after its hashes and this has "
            + "none, so the line is a paragraph and the hash is a sigil."),

        new("Quoted as syntax", "code-span", "Write `#sync` to tag something.", "not a tag",
            "The code-span alternative is matched first and nothing inside one is inline-parsed, so a tag being "
            + "talked about stays text.")
    ];

    private const string PlacesSample = """
        A paragraph mentioning #sync and #markdown in passing.

        - A bullet with #docs on it
        - [x] A finished task, tagged #aspire
        - [ ] An unfinished one, tagged #ui

        > A quote can carry #sync too.

        | Area | Tags |
        |---|---|
        | Editing | #markdown #ui |
        | Sync | #sync |
        """;

    private const string InertSample = """
        This paragraph has #sync in it. Try to press it: there is no tab stop, no
        cursor change and nothing behind it.
        """;

    private const string PatternCode = """
        @* One alternative of MarkdownPreview's inline pattern. The lookbehind is
           what makes the hash a sigil rather than a character — it has to open a
           word — and the letter after it is what keeps `#1` a number. *@
        @"|(?<tag>(?<!\S)#[A-Za-z][\w-]*)"

        @* And the record it becomes. The stored text keeps the hash: the tag is
           what the author typed, and the chip is only how it is drawn. *@
        else if (match.Groups["tag"].Success) parts.Add(new MdTag(value[1..]));
        """;

    private const string RenderCode = """
        @* MarkdownRender's MdTag arm, in full. A span, a class, and the text with
           its hash put back on. *@
        case MdTag tag:
            Wrap(builder, ref seq, "span", "tag-chip", $"#{tag.Tag}");
            break;

        @* And in the plain-text reading, which is what a copy button copies. *@
        MdTag tag => $"#{tag.Tag}",
        """;

    private const string DifferenceCode = """
        @* The inline: what the renderer does with an MdTag. Note what is not
           here — no TagChip, no label element, no click, no remove. *@
        case MdTag tag:
            Wrap(builder, ref seq, "span", "tag-chip", $"#{tag.Tag}");
            break;

        @* The component, for the same tag, in its three forms. The hash is the
           caller's: TagChip prints Text and adds nothing to it. *@
        <TagChip Text="#sync" />
        <TagChip Text="#sync" OnClick="Filter" />
        <TagChip Text="#sync" Removable="true" OnRemove="Drop" />
        """;

    private const string InertCode = """
        @* Nothing to pass. There is no tag hook on MarkdownView and no parameter
           on MarkdownRender for one — the inline-target hook takes code spans and
           links, and a tag is neither. *@
        <MarkdownView Blocks="@MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(body)" />

        @* Where a tag is a control instead. Each of these is documented on its own
           page; this is only which page to go to. *@
        <TagChip Text="@tag" OnClick="Filter" Removable="true" OnRemove="Drop" />
        <TagMultiSelect Values="@selected" ValuesChanged="Replace" Options="@known" />
        """;
}
